WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
Answering hard questions.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:05.336
The defense is where a reviewer tests whether the report holds up.

3
00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:07.280
Preparation is most of it.

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.181
Write your answers in advance.

5
00:00:09.461 --> 00:00:12.341
The project page lists likely reviewer questions.

6
00:00:12.621 --> 00:00:18.296
Answer each in writing, with a pointer to the evidence (a table row, a log entry, a test).

7
00:00:18.576 --> 00:00:22.949
Writing exposes the answers you don’t have while there is still time to get them.

8
00:00:23.229 --> 00:00:25.213
Run a premortem on your own defense.

9
00:00:25.493 --> 00:00:31.680
Klein’s premortem works here too: imagine the defense went badly, and list the questions that sank it.

10
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.989
Typical ones: “what did the baseline score on held-out?

11
00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:37.253
”, “how many prompt variants did you try?

12
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:40.435
”, “what happens if the retrieved document is the attack?

13
00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:41.888
” Then answer them.

14
00:00:42.168 --> 00:00:44.408
Separate what you measured from what you believe.

15
00:00:44.688 --> 00:00:48.336
The strongest answers sound like: “We measured X, with this interval.

16
00:00:48.616 --> 00:00:51.517
We believe Y, because of Z, but we didn’t test it.

17
00:00:51.797 --> 00:00:54.123
” Reviewers are not looking for certainty.

18
00:00:54.403 --> 00:00:59.459
They are checking whether you know which of your statements are evidence and which are hypotheses.

19
00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:02.789
“I don’t know, and here’s how I’d find out” is a good answer.

20
00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:04.669
It is far better than a guess.

21
00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:11.051
A guess delivered confidently, and later found wrong, costs you the credibility of everything else you said.

22
00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:12.952
Concede, then quantify.

23
00:01:13.232 --> 00:01:25.221
When a reviewer finds a real weakness, agree, and size it: “Yes, the eval set has only nine scanned-PDF cases, so that slice’s interval is wide, roughly 40 to 90 percent.

24
00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.229
” Then say what you would do about it.

25
00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:28.811
Record what you learn.

26
00:01:29.091 --> 00:01:32.931
Note every question you couldn’t answer well and every change you would make.

27
00:01:33.211 --> 00:01:37.499
If you had a peer or mentor review, list what they asked and what you changed.

28
00:01:37.779 --> 00:01:43.923
That list is part of the defense checklist, and in a real team it is the start of the next design review.
